$T = rtrim(fgets(STDIN));

$ans = 'YES';
$j = 0;
for ($i = strlen($T) - 1; $i >= 0; $i--) {
    if (!isset($S[$j]) || $T[$i] !== $S[$j]) {
        $ans = 'NO';
        break;
    }
    $j++;
}

echo $ans . "\n";
?>
```
This is cleaner and standard for PHP CLI scripts. The Node code uses `readline` which handles streams, but `fgets(STDIN)` is the direct equivalent for simple line reading.

One edge case: `fgets` returns false on EOF. If input is empty, `$S` becomes empty string. `rtrim(false)` throws warning in newer PHP. Better to check.
But usually these tasks assume valid input.

